WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.429
Today’s guest video is from
Amy at Vintage Space,

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:05.020
who has brought along one of the greatest

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.700
and also the tiniest props that I have ever seen

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.470
Amy, it’s all yours.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.160
One of the benefits to being a professional
space history nerd

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.160
is you get to add some amazing artefacts to
your private collection.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.080
My latest addition is this tiny piece of Kapton
foil from Apollo 11.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.320
This tiny piece of foil went to the moon and
came back through the earth’s atmosphere

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.250
to be put on to this piece of paper, so I
can hang it on my wall.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.760
But if you look closely at the piece, you’ll
notice that it’s sort of silvery

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.030
and it looks like it’s been scratched off
and there’s gold underneath.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.780
So, what’s going on with this piece of foil?

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.600
What exactly was that silvery stuff?

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.679
And the larger question is, why does the Apollo
spacecraft look white when it launches,

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.839
silver when it’s around the moon,

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:50.350
and a dirty gold once it’s come back to
earth?

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.299
Well, it’s all part of the thermal protection
system.

00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:58.140
Thermal protection was a huge engineering
challenge for the men and woman who designed

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.160
and built the Apollo spacecraft.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.350
Not only was the vehicle going to be flying
through the vacuum of space

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.149
where it would be exposed to raw, unfiltered
sunlight

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:10.910
and also in the shadow behind the moon, where
there would be no light hitting it at all:

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:15.490
it also had to return through the earth’s
atmosphere at lunar re-entry speeds.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.700
This is an insanely fast re-entry through
the earth’s atmosphere.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:20.950
In the case of Apollo,

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:30.810
this re-entry speed was 24,677 miles per hour
or 36,194 feet per second.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:36.530
That translated to a heat during re-entry
of over 4,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.240
That meant the Apollo spacecraft
was going to need

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.440
a very specific thermal protection system

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.560
to not only get the spacecraft through the
earth’s atmosphere in one piece,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.470
but to protect the three astronauts inside
it.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.479
The thermal protection system actually involved
a number of different elements.

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:53.499
First was the boost protective cover.

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:56.130
This is the white covering that fits over
the command module

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.720
that you see as being white when the spacecraft
has made it to its launch vehicle

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.200
on the launch pad, ready to go to the moon.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.540
This protected the spacecraft from the heat
associated with launching through the atmosphere,

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:08.110
but it didn’t last very long.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.740
The boost protective cover was separated from
the spacecraft

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.720
when the launch escape tower jettisoned 200
seconds after launch.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.490
Once the boost protective cover was clear,
the spacecraft looked silver.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:23.090
That’s because the entire Apollo command
module was covered with a pressure sensitive

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.920
Kapton polyimide tape.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.970
This tape was made of coded aluminium and
oxidized silicon monoxide.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.480
The tape was applied to the entire external
structure of the command module

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.530
with the gold side down and the silver side
facing out.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.530
That’s why,

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.520
when you see pictures of the Apollo spacecraft
in orbit around the moon,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.530
it looks like it’s reflecting space in a
mirror.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:43.530
It’s beautiful.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:47.060
Now, when it came time for the Apollo spacecraft
to re-enter the atmosphere,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:52.230
this Kapton tape wasn’t exactly enough to
protect it from the searing heats of re-entry

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.970
and it wasn’t designed to.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:54.980
The Apollo spacecraft,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.050
like the Mercury and Gemini vehicles before
it, was a blunt body design.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.990
It had a rounded bottom and a conical after
body,

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.770
designed to minimize convective heating during
re-entry.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.620
The centre of gravity in the vehicle was also
offset from the axis of symmetry

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.890
in order to generate a very small amount of
lift.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.500
This meant that the spacecraft had a little
bit of controllability during re-entry.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.090
So, the blunt underside of the spacecraft
was taking the brunt of the heat

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.990
and that was where the last piece of the thermal
protective system was housed.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.520
That of course is the ablative heat shield.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.040
The heat shield was a honeycomb structure
bonded to the stainless-steel body of the

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.040
spacecraft

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.870
and each little honeycomb was individually
filled with an ablative material

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.830
that would burn away as it was heated

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:40.510
thus protecting the spacecraft from the worst
of heat of re-entry.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.960
But, the heat shield and the bow shock generated
by that blunt body of re-entry

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.140
couldn’t entirely protect the outer structure
of the spacecraft.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.520
As it went through that fireball phase of
re-entry the Kapton was burned away a little bit,

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.840
revealing the golden underside of the tape.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:01.900
But how is it that I’m able to actually
own a piece of the Apollo 11 spacecraft?

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.380
Well, it turns out that recovery crews and
pretty much anybody

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.970
who could get within arm’s reach of the
spacecraft,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.030
and this doesn’t just go for Apollo 11,

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.640
this applies to all the vehicles that went
to the moon,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.660
they would reach up and just take off a tiny
piece of that foil as a souvenir.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:22.520
So, even if at first glance it looks like
a tiny insignificant dime-sized portion of foil,

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:27.060
it’s actually amazing to think that that
little dime-sized piece of foil

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.470
helped to protect the Apollo 11 astronauts
on the way to the moon

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.819
and their re-entry back through the earth’s
atmosphere.

00:04:33.819 --> 00:04:36.860
Go subscribe to Amy’s channel for all sorts
of videos about space flight!

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:41.460
I would recommend starting with her one on
how the Saturn V got to the launch pad.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.190
Next time, we have a video about linguistics
and music all in one.

